+### Bridge helper workflow
+
+Use this workflow when the browser owner has supplied a verified browser WebSocket endpoint and authorized the specific existing page target. Metadata discovery is separate from page attachment:
+
+```bash
+agent-browser cdp targets "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>" --json
+```
+
+After the owner authorizes the returned page target, put the verified endpoint and exact target ID in a dedicated `bridge.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "session": "bridge",
+  "cdp": "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>",
+  "attachTarget": "<CDP-id>",
+  "strictRefs": true
+}
+```
+
+Pass that same config on every command, including after a daemon restart:
+
+```bash
+# Establish the initial connection explicitly using the configured endpoint
+agent-browser --config ./bridge.json connect "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>"
+agent-browser --config ./bridge.json snapshot --redact-editable-values
+```
+
+`cdp targets` is standalone: it sends only `Browser.getVersion` and `Target.getTargets`, does not start a daemon or browser, and never attaches to a page. Its JSON envelope is `{success: true, data: {browser, targets}}`; `browser` contains `product` and `protocolVersion`, and each target contains `targetId`, `type`, and optional safe `origin`. Titles, URL paths, queries, and fragments are omitted. A failed or empty target list fails closed; do not guess a target or fall back to automatic discovery.
+
+This metadata command accepts only `ws://` URLs whose literal host is `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`, with an explicit port and a browser-level `/devtools/browser` or `/devtools/browser/<id>` path. Hostnames such as `localhost`, remote hosts, credentials, query strings, and fragments are rejected. These restrictions are specific to metadata discovery; the general `--cdp` connection forms above remain available. Loopback `ws://` transport is unencrypted, and a local address does not establish ownership or authorization. The owner must verify the endpoint and authorize the target before use.
+
+`--attach-target` chooses an existing `page` target for explicit `--cdp` or `connect`. It creates no startup tab and fails if that target is missing rather than selecting another tab. Actual attachment enables page domains and performs normal page initialization, so it is not metadata-only. This differs from stock `--pin-tab`, which creates its own initial tab when no binding exists. An explicit `tab new` command remains allowed; creating a tab must still be within the authorized task.
+
+`attachTarget` selects the initial existing target. An explicit, authorized `tab new` or `tab <id>` updates the current session binding; later commands using the same bridge config keep that chosen tab rather than returning to the initial target. An explicit same-endpoint `connect` with `attachTarget` may select the specified authorized target while reusing the healthy WebSocket. It cannot clear a blocked connection; only an explicitly authorized `reconnect` starts a new connection attempt.
+
+Start a fresh named session to enable `--strict-refs`; a non-strict daemon or existing live browser, inspector, or stream cannot be reused. Strict mode requires an explicit existing target or opt-in `--discover-existing` and rejects debug mode, providers, state replay/restore, plugins, and initialization overrides. Strict mode rejects raw export/inspection paths including `eval`, `get value`, `inspect`, `network`, `cookies`, `state`, `record`, `press`, `read`, `chat`, `dashboard`, and stream enable, and disables daemon streaming listeners.
+
+`--strict-refs` keeps refs scoped to the current target and snapshot generation. Use the exact ref emitted by the latest redacted snapshot; never reconstruct one or replace a failed ref with a role, name, or CSS selector. Take a fresh snapshot after relevant navigation or a tab switch. See [Snapshot Options](#snapshot-options) and the [bridge helper reference](skill-data/core/references/bridge-helper.md) for the stricter session rules.
+
+Strict commands are sent at most once. After an uncertain transport write or read, the CLI does not retry or respawn the daemon; it returns the stable, static `outcome_unknown` error. The action may already have happened. Inspect the current state and take a fresh redacted snapshot before deciding what to do next; do not blindly repeat mutating commands such as `click`, `type`, or `tab new`.
+
+In strict mode, `session info` reports cached diagnostics through the normal daemon identity and configuration gates, without a WebSocket connection, liveness probe, or target attachment. Numeric fields are `connect_attempts`, `explicit_reconnects`, `target_attach_attempts`, `target_set_auto_attach_attempts`, `auto_attached_events`, and `daemon_starts` (1 for the current process). It also returns `connection_blocked` as a boolean and `permission_pending: null`: a generic timeout cannot establish whether browser consent is pending. These diagnostics are not a replacement for standalone target discovery.
+
+### Discover existing targets on a retained connection
+
+For target selection after the browser connection has been authorized, use opt-in `--discover-existing` (config `discoverExisting: true`). It requires `--strict-refs` and an explicit browser-level CDP endpoint, and cannot be combined with `--attach-target`. It does not discover an endpoint, launch a browser, or select an arbitrary page.
+
+Use a fresh named session and a dedicated `discover.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "session": "bridge-discovery",
+  "cdp": "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>",
+  "discoverExisting": true,
+  "strictRefs": true
+}
+```
+
+```bash
+agent-browser --config ./discover.json connect "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>"
+agent-browser --config ./discover.json tab list --json
+# After the owner authorizes one returned page target
+agent-browser --config ./discover.json tab <target-id>
+agent-browser --config ./discover.json snapshot --redact-editable-values
+```
+
+Pass the same dedicated config on every command. Initial `connect` retains one browser WebSocket and performs only target metadata and lifecycle discovery. It does not attach a page, enable `Runtime` or `Page` domains, call `Target.setAutoAttach`, or create a startup blank tab. `tab list` explicitly refreshes safe metadata over that retained socket: target IDs, types, and optional safe origins, without titles or URL paths, queries, or fragments. Zero targets leaves the connection open for a later explicit `tab list`; there is no fallback or automatic page selection.
+
+Until an explicit `tab <target-id>` selects an authorized existing `page`, snapshots, page actions, and `tab new` fail with `TARGET_SELECTION_REQUIRED`. `tab list`, `session info`, explicit target selection, and `close` remain available. Initial selection attaches and enables the chosen page once, then saves its binding using cached metadata without focusing it or reading page content; later explicit tab switches retain stock behavior. Later commands and same-endpoint `connect` in that live session preserve the chosen binding. A fresh discovery connection after a daemon restart remains unselected even if a previous binding exists, so select the target explicitly again. Cached healthy `session info` and status diagnostics send zero CDP queries. Failed or pending strict connections remain blocked without automatic retry; the 25-second handshake limit is unchanged. Require `existing-target-discovery-v1` before using this workflow.
+
+### Bridge connection lifetime
+
+Strict sessions require an initial explicit `connect <endpoint>` before snapshot, tab, or page commands. The dedicated config and `--cdp` identify the connection; routine commands never establish one implicitly. Healthy same-endpoint `connect`, status, and page commands retain the existing WebSocket. They do not send CDP launch preludes or use the three-second liveness-probe recovery path. The initial strict WebSocket handshake timeout is 25 seconds; standalone metadata diagnostics retain their 5-second timeout. Timeout or unknown outcome never authorizes an automatic retry.
+
+A failed, pending, or timed-out connection or attachment, or a closed connection, records a durable, payload-free `connection_blocked` marker. Later commands and daemon restarts cannot automatically reopen that connection. Only the distinct `reconnect <endpoint>` command explicitly authorizes another connection attempt, clearing the previous block for that attempt only. Never call it automatically after an error, and never automate browser consent UI. Endpoint and existing-target authorization requirements still apply.
+
+```bash
+# Only after explicit authorization for a new connection attempt
+agent-browser --config ./bridge.json reconnect "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/<browser-id>"
+# Continue only if reconnect succeeded
+agent-browser --config ./bridge.json snapshot --redact-editable-values
+```
+
+`cdp targets` is diagnostic discovery: it opens a separate WebSocket on every invocation. During an approved connected lifetime, use `agent-browser --config ./bridge.json tab list` over the retained WebSocket instead of repeated metadata probes. Discovery never clears a connection block or authorizes reconnection.
+
+### Private input and local verification
+
+For private input, launch the executable directly without a shell and write a single JSON object to stdin:
+
+```bash
+agent-browser --config /absolute/path/bridge.json --json bridge-command --stdin
+```
+
+The stdin body has exactly one field, for example `{"argv":["fill","@exact-ref","private input"]}`. Use the exact ref from the latest redacted snapshot. The dedicated config must explicitly set `strictRefs: true`, a named non-default `session`, the CDP endpoint, and either `attachTarget` or `discoverExisting: true`. Input is limited to 65,536 UTF-8 bytes; unknown fields, trailing objects, non-string arguments, nested wrappers, batch commands, global configuration flags, and plugins are rejected. Inner commands use the canonical parser and strict allowlist. There is no shell execution or raw export escape hatch, and private arguments are not echoed. Write private values through a host stdin pipe rather than putting them in helper or shell process arguments.
+
+#### Local GUI target labels
+
+A trusted local GUI adapter can request bounded page titles solely for its local target picker. Send exactly this private stdin envelope through the native CLI `bridge-command --stdin` wrapper used by the local GUI, with the same dedicated strict config:
+
+```json
+{"argv":["tab","list","--local-labels"]}
+```
+
+The strict wrapper validates this envelope, and the canonical parser permits `--local-labels` only on this exact inner `tab list` command. Direct CLI argument forms such as `tab list --local-labels`, generic MCP `extraArgs`, and the MCP private `agent_browser_bridge_command` tool all reject it. There is no global flag, config key, or environment variable for local labels. Require `local-target-labels-v1`.
+
+This projection uses the retained browser WebSocket without attaching a page. It adds a `title` of at most 512 Unicode characters with standard controls and bidirectional formatting characters, including U+061C, removed, alongside the existing safe origin, `targetId`, `type`, and `active` metadata. Titles may contain private information; keep this response in the local GUI picker, never forward its titles over WSS or put them in server logs. Ordinary strict `tab list`, snapshots, and status remain title-free; this option does not enable title output on those surfaces.
+
+#### Local predicate verification
+
+The inner command `verify [--expected-url URL] [--expected-text TEXT] [--ref STRICT_REF --expected-value VALUE]` is accepted only through this wrapper. Supply at least one assertion. URL comparison uses the complete URL with trailing `/` characters removed from both sides; text uses whitespace-normalized containment on both sides; value comparison is exact, including an empty expected value. All supplied assertions are ANDed. Pair `--ref` with `--expected-value`, using a fresh exact strict ref. Value checks support native input, textarea, and select value getters in an isolated world and fail closed without selector or getter fallback; contenteditable elements are unsupported.
+
+For example, send `{"argv":["verify","--ref","@exact-ref","--expected-value","private input"]}` as the stdin body. The response's `data` is exactly `{"matched":true}` or `{"matched":false}`; actual URLs, body text, and field values are not returned. Errors are static. A mismatch is a completed check, and checks do not make page changes atomic. Existing snapshot redaction still is not a general secret scrubber: unrelated prose and labels can expose secrets. See the [bridge helper reference](skill-data/core/references/bridge-helper.md) for the full input contract and MCP examples.
+
+Successful strict mutations include `completed: true`; verification returns only its `matched` boolean. For action failures, `data.executed: false` identifies an explicit pre-handler guard rejection: a strict-ref guard, `TARGET_SELECTION_REQUIRED`, or invalid initial target selection. Handler/post-dispatch failures use `data.executed: null`, meaning the action may have dispatched. Missing execution metadata is not proof that nothing happened. Do not automatically replay after an error or `outcome_unknown`; inspect current state and refresh refs before deciding what to do next.
+
+### Bridge capability detection
+
+`agent-browser --version --json` returns a structured object:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": "0.38.2",
+  "bridgeProtocol": 1,
+  "capabilities": [
+    "metadata-targets-v1",
+    "existing-target-v1",
+    "existing-target-discovery-v1",
+    "local-target-labels-v1",
+    "strict-refs-v1",
+    "redact-editable-values-v1",
+    "private-command-v1",
+    "local-verify-v1",
+    "persistent-session-v1",
+    "crowdworks-inspection-v1"
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Human-readable `agent-browser --version` still reports the upstream version; the helper daemon identity uses `+bridge.4`. `bridgeProtocol` remains `1`. Integrations should inspect the bridge protocol and required capabilities rather than treating the upstream version alone as proof of these additions.
+
+### Read-only CrowdWorks observation
+
+Require `crowdworks-inspection-v1` and send the argument-free private stdin envelope `{"argv":["crowdworks-inspect"]}` through `agent-browser --config /absolute/path/bridge.json --json bridge-command --stdin`. The canonical action is `crowdworks_inspect`; MCP uses `agent_browser_bridge_command` with the same `configPath` and `argv`, intentionally without a separate tool. Direct command arguments and extra inspection options are rejected.
+
+The existing strict exact target must already be selected, with no active iframe scope. Supported pages are HTTPS `crowdworks.jp` or `www.crowdworks.jp`, standard HTTPS port, no userinfo, at `/public/jobs` or its slash-delimited subtree. The command retains the existing WebSocket, validates target/session and document before and after, and never selects another target, creates a tab, wakes/focuses a renderer, or retries. It captures one main-frame AX tree and one pierced DOM tree on the selected session, with a fixed number of CDP calls independent of card count. That single DOM capture may contain descendant iframe/shadow metadata solely for safe mapping and redaction; main/light-DOM describes the output projection, not every raw captured node. No separate frame or child-session query is made. The existing isolated-world document handle is used only for identity; no general evaluation or page getter is added.
+
+Success `data` contains exactly `origin`, `candidates`, `text`, and `coverage`. Each `cw_0`, `cw_1`, and subsequent candidate contains only `href`, `label`, and `context`. These are observation IDs, never action refs. Job hrefs are canonical `https://crowdworks.jp/public/jobs/<ASCII digits>` with queries, fragments and suffixes removed. Labels come from each anchor's own projected accessible name. Context comes from projected nonignored AX names in DOM order in the nearest `tr`, role `row`, `li`, role `listitem`, `article`, or proved parent element; neighboring headings and budgets are never inferred. Native AX URL properties are correlated by backend DOM ID; if absent, only a main light-DOM HTML `A[href]` resolved against the captured document `baseURL` is supported. Contradictory evidence fails closed. A selected card range containing different canonical job URLs is unsupported, including shared semantic ancestors and fallback parents; repeated anchors for the same job remain supported. AX child/parent links and root reachability must prove a complete in-scope graph. Related labels need nonignored AX evidence, and aggregate names crossing excluded scopes need active safe provenance.
+
+Editable values, editable descendant echoes and related value-derived names reuse safe snapshot redaction before any tree aggregation. RootWebArea titles, InlineTextBox duplicates, iframe/contentDocument, shadow, template and hidden scopes are excluded. Raw document titles, full page URLs, HTML, DOM attributes and AX values never enter the result. Unrelated page prose and accessible labels are still untrusted data and can contain secrets; this is not a general secret scrubber.
+
+Coverage is exactly `{"complete":true,"frame_scope":"main","shadow_scope":"none","candidate_count":N,"truncated":false,"unscanned":false}`. Fixed budgets are 20,000 DOM nodes, 20,000 AX nodes, DOM/AX depth 64, 4 MiB scanned string/key data per capture (with conservative scalar charges; not a wire-size claim), 200 candidates, 4,096 UTF-8 bytes per label, 16,000 per context, 128 KiB of page text, 512 KiB summed page text and candidate href/label/context data, and 786,432 UTF-8 bytes of serialized JSON `data` (including escaping). No text is sliced: overflow returns `CROWDWORKS_SCOPE_OVERFLOW` with no partial observation, preserving negative escrow evidence past the former 10,000-character boundary. Missing scope, mapping or URL evidence returns `CROWDWORKS_SCOPE_UNSUPPORTED`; a genuinely empty supported page can succeed. Capture/transport/document races return `CROWDWORKS_INSPECTION_UNAVAILABLE`, taking precedence over projection errors after the post-capture guard. The command skips mutating queued-iframe maintenance; ordinary commands and the daemon’s existing background behavior are unchanged.
+
+Read-only scope/overflow failures and the action-specific argument gate explicitly return `data.executed:false`, so callers may preserve the approved connection. Transport/document failures remain `data.executed:null`; never infer non-execution from an error code or retry automatically. Ordinary snapshot URL rejection and local GUI-only title privacy are unchanged. Mock CDP tests establish this contract; real CrowdWorks rendering has not been observed, and equivalence to the previous extension's raw DOM projection is not claimed. Consumers must require complete coverage and stop on unsupported/overflow rather than interpreting them as an empty listing or proof of escrow.
+
