+Strict configuration must be supplied per MCP tool call. Start plain `agent-browser mcp`; `--strict-refs mcp` and `--config bridge.json mcp` when that config sets `strictRefs: true` are rejected with a static startup error. For private input, call `agent_browser_bridge_command` with `configPath` (the dedicated strict config), `argv` (array of strings), and optional `timeoutMs`. For predicate checks, call `agent_browser_verify` with `configPath`, one or more of `expectedUrl`, `expectedText`, or paired `ref`/`expectedValue`, and optional `timeoutMs`. Both use the same CLI stdin wrapper and canonical parser; private values stay out of helper process arguments. Neither accepts arbitrary `extraArgs`. MCP requests still contain these values, so protect client/server request logs.
+
+Local GUI picker titles are available only through the native CLI private stdin wrapper used by the local GUI. MCP `agent_browser_bridge_command` rejects `["tab", "list", "--local-labels"]`, and generic MCP `extraArgs` also rejects local labels. No MCP common `localLabels` field exists. Keep the bounded-title response local to the GUI; never forward titles over WSS or into server logs. Require `local-target-labels-v1` for the native local projection.
+
+On ordinary browser tool calls, pass `extraArgs: ["--config", "/absolute/path/bridge.json"]`, or `strictRefs: true` plus the same explicit `session` and `cdp`, and either `attachTarget` or `discoverExisting: true`. Snapshots also need `redactEditableValues: true`. Do not rely on inherited or sticky MCP server startup configuration.
